## INTRODUCTION
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are a big family of crystalline porous materials, consisting of inorganic metal clusters and organic linkers.1 Owing to their designable structures and huge potentials in many applications, MOFs have aroused considerable interest of scientists in various fields, such as gas adsorption,2 sensing,3 proton conduction,4 heterogeneous catalysis,5 light emitting,6 and biochemical systems.7 In principle, MOFs are capable of satisfying a great variety of requirements based on the synthetic and reticular chemistry.8 The high tunability of surface chemistry environment and pore textural properties, such as surface area, pore size, and pore shape, enables the generation of a great many of MOFs and the ability to tailor their characteristic properties for various applications. MOFs can be predesigned to incorporate various ligands that not only act as inert scaffolds, but also contain functional groups that have certain interactions with particular guest molecules. This strategy has been successfully employed to design MOFs that can bind specific molecules and further transform the reactivity of these guests under certain conditions.9
In supramolecular systems, the molecular recognition through encapsulation processes is primarily determined by the volumes of host and guest molecules. According to Rebek’s rule, the strong binding usually occurs when the volume ratio of guest/host molecules is in the range of $\mathbf{0.55{\overset{\cdot}{=}}0.09}$ .10 Based on this rule, many host-guest systems have been designed and successfully developed, giving rise to a series of new strategies to encapsulate macromolecules, such as proteins, nucleic acid, and fullerene derivatives.  In addition, it’s been reported that electronpoor hosts tend to bind even electron-poor guests with the high affinity and selectivity.11 For example, Nitschke and coworkers have successfully developed an electrondeficient cubic $\mathrm{Fe}_{8}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage, wherein the incorporation of perfluorophenylene units greatly enhanced its binding ability to specific functionalized fullerenes. Based on Rebek’s rule, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{8}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage can selectively encapsulate $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}$ - indene and $C_{60}$ -anthracene bisadducts instead of unfunctionalized fullerenes and monoadducts. Inspired by this work, we attempted to apply this concept to MOFs. The great structural similarity may trigger selective binding of fullerene derivatives in MOFs. Considering the high stability and insolubility of pyrazolide-based MOFs, it’s quite promising to develop MOFs as recyclable heterogeneous catalysts for the synthesis of fullerene adducts. In particular, the fluorine atom has the highest electronegativity and exceptionally poor polarizability, polyfluorinated materials usually exhibit unique physicochemical properties.12 The distinct electron deficiency of fluorinated compounds endow them with specific fluorophilicity that promotes electro-poor self-aggregation/segregation.13
Fullerenes are well known as a class of carbon allotropes consisting of molecules composed totally of carbon atoms in the forms of hollow spheres or ellipsoids.14 Due to their remarkable electronic properties and solution processability, many derivatives of fullerenes have been widely utilized in material synthesis,15 electronics,16 biomedical systems,17 nanotechnologies,18 and solar energy conversion19. However, owing to the high symmetry of its spherical structures and the existence of multifold reacScheme 1. Synthetic routes of TTFPPPa
tive sites, it remains a big challenge to selectively synthesize fullerenes with a certain number of functional units. Although several synthetic strategies have been developed to implement their functionalization, such as cyclopropanations,20a 1,3-dipolar cycloadditions,20b and DielsAlder
cycloadditions,20c these reactions generally produce a mixture of many fullerene derivatives, which are very difficult to be separated from each other.21 In response to the challenges for selective synthesis of fullerene derivatives, we rationally designed and synthesized a MOF, namely PCN624, which is isostructural to PCN-602 with an ftw-a topology (Figure 1a, b).22  Firstly, the incorporation of electron-deficient perfluorophenylene units into MOFs could selectively enhance its binding ability to electron-poor fullerenes. Secondly, unique combination of the favorable pore size, pore shape and pore volume provides additional driving force for the encapsulation of fullerene derivatives in MOFs. Finally, the network of PCN-624 constructed by $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\right]$ cluster and pyrazolide-based porphyrin ligands were proven to have high chemical stability, which endows $\mathrm{PCN-}624$ with long-term recycling stability. The feasible PCN-624 nanoreactor could realize the control over the reactivity of fullerenes and selective formation of desired fullerene adducts.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials. The commercial chemicals are purchased unless mentioned otherwise. $^{1,4}$ -Dioxane, pyrrole, trifluoroacetic acid (TFA), $^{2,3}$ -dichloro- $^{\cdot5,6}$ -dicyano-1,4- benzoquinone (DDQ), potassium carbonate $\left(\operatorname{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}\right)$ , pentafluorobenzaldehyde, lithium bromide, and triphenylmethyl chloride were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Corporation. Nickel(II) acetylacetonate $\left(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}\right)$ , nickel(II) acetate tetrahydrate $({\mathrm{Ni}}({\mathrm{OAc}})_{2})$ , fullerene $\left(\mathrm{C}_{60}\right)$ , anthracene and tetrakis(triphenylphosphine)palladium $\mathrm{(Pd(PPh}_{3})_{4})$ were purchased from  Tokyo Kasai Co. (TCI). 4-(4,4,5,5-Tetramethyl-1,3,2-dioxaborolan- $^{\cdot2}$ -yl)- $.1H.$ - pyrazole was purchased from Ark Pharm Incorporation. The precipitate was collected by centrifugation and washed with anhydrous $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) three times and acetone twice.
Instrumentation. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) experiments were carried out on a BRUKER $\mathrm{D}_{8}$ -Focus Bragg−Brentano $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray powder. Diffractometer equipped with a Cu sealed tube $(\protect\hat{\lambda}=1.54178\textrm{\AA})$ at 40 kV and $40~\mathrm{{mA}}$ . Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) data were collected on a Mercury 400 NMR spectrometer. The simulated PXRD spectra were obtained by the diffraction-crystal module of the Mercury program based on the singlecrystal data. The program is available free of charge via the Internet at http:/www.iucr.org. FT-IR spectra were recorded on an IR Affinity-1 instrument. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) data were obtained on a TGA-50 (SHIMADZU) thermogravimetric analyzer with a heating rate of 10 $^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}\ \operatorname*{min}^{-1}$ under air atmosphere. $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption−desorption isotherms were measured using a Micrometritics ASAP 2020 system at $77~\mathrm{K}.$ . The samples were activated by supercritical carbon dioxide using MADRIDE prior to gas adsorption. The UV−vis absorption spectra were recorded on a Shimadzu UV-2450 spectrophotometer with a wavelength of $350~\mathrm{nm}$ . The product was identified with Shimadzu/Kratos Axima CFR MALDI-TOF PSD instrument of moderate resolution and mass accuracy. The conversions of the reactants and the yields of products were measured by Shimadzu LC-2030C system equipped with a UV/Vis detector.
Synthesis of 5,10,15,20-tetrakis $(2,3,5,6$ -tetrafluoro-4- ( $\mathbf{\Delta}_{:}H.$ -pyrazol- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{4}$ -yl)phenyl)-porphyrin (TTFPPP). The synthesis route and details are described in Scheme 1 and Supporting Information Section 2.
Synthesis of PCN-624. ${\mathrm{Ni}}(\mathrm{AcO})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{(8~\mg,}$ , 32.3 mmol), TTFPPP(10 mg, $\mathbf{17.4~mmol}_{,}$ ), and deionized (DI) water $\left(1.2{\mathrm{~mL}}\right)$ in $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide were mixed and ultrasonically dissolved in a 5 mL highpressure vessel. The resulting mixture was sealed and heated at 120 $^{\circ}C$ for $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the vial cooled down to room temperature, purple crystalline powder of PCN-624 was collected by filtration $(9\mathrm{mg})$ ).
Gas Adsorption of PCN-624. Before the gas sorption experiment, as-synthesized PCN-624 $\bf{\tilde{\alpha}}{\sim}{100}\mathrm{~\mg})$ was washed twice with DMF and acetone once, respectively. Fresh acetone was subsequently added, and the sample was soaked for $_{24}\mathrm{h}$ to exchange and remove the nonvolatile solvates (DMF). After the removal of acetone by decanting, the samples were activated by drying under vacuum, then dried again by using the “outgas” function of the adsorption instrument for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ at 100 $^{\circ}C$ prior to gas adsorption/desorption measurement. Then, their $\Nu_{2}$ uptake was measured at $77~\mathrm{K}$ .
PXRD Measurement for Stability Test of PCN-624. After being washed with DMF and acetone, as-obtained PCN-624 sample (100 mg for each batch) were immersed in about 10 mL aqueous
solutions of HCl $\left(\mathbf{1}~\mathbf{M}\right)$ aqueous solution, $\Nu_{4_{2}C O_{3}}$ $(_{3}~\mathrm{{M})}$ aqueous solution, and ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ ${\bf\Pi}(_{3}\mathrm{~M})$ aqueous solution at room temperature for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. The treated samples were collected and washed successively with DI water $(3{\times}5~\mathrm{mL})$ and acetone $(3\ \times5\ \mathrm{\mL})$ ). The obtained powders were dried in an oven at 100 $^{\circ}C$ before PXRD measurements.
$\mathbf{N_{2}}$ Adsorption for Stability Test of PCN-624. Five batches of PCN-624 samples (about 100 mg for each) were immersed in 10 mL of HCl $\mathbf{\Gamma}(\mathbf{1}\mathbf{M})$ , $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\left(3\mathrm{M}\right)$ , and ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ $(_{3}\mathrm{~M})$ aqueous solutions at room temperature for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. After being collected and washed with DI water $(3{\times}5~\mathrm{mL})$ and acetone $(3{\times}5~\mathrm{mL})$ , the samples were activated under vacuum at 100 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 $\mathbf{h}$ on ASAP 2020 system. These activated samples were then measured for $\Nu_{2}$ sorption at 77 K.
Encapsulation Studies. In a typical experiment, PCN$624$ $\mathrm{(2o~mg)}$ was dispersed into a toluene $(_{3}\mathrm{mL})$ solution of different fullerene derivatives $(2.0~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}})$ under a nitrogen atmosphere in a $4~\mathrm{mL}$ vial. The electronic absorption spectra of the solution were recorded after stirring for every $^2\mathrm{~h~}$ and standing for 10 min at room temperature.
The  adsorption  of  fullerenes  was  then  recorded  and used  to  calculate  the  amount  of fullerene derivatives that were adsorbed by PCN-624 according to LambertBeer’s Law.
Heterogeneous Catalysis. Anthracene $(9.9{\mathrm{~mg}}$ , 55.5 $\mathsf{\upmu m o l})$ and $C_{60}$ (20 mg, $27.7~\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ ) were added into $5~\mathrm{ml}$ acetonitrile in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ pressure tube. $P C N-624$ (100 mg) was dispersed into the mixture and heated at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 h under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere. After the completion of the reaction, the precipitate was collected by filtration and washed with toluene $(2{\times}5~\mathrm{{mL})}$ ) and $\mathrm{CS}_{2}$ $\left(3{\times}5~\mathrm{mL}\right)$ ). The remaining solid was dried under vacuum and then soaked in a toluene/ $\mathrm{^CS}_{2}$ mixture (1:1, $3{\times}10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . After filtration, the combined filtrate was collected and evaporated the solvent under vacuum. Finally, the identities and the yields of the products were determined by HPLC-MS with $\mathrm{C}_{70}$ as an internal standard (detailed information is in section $^3$ of Supporting Information).
Recycle of Catalyst. The used $P C N-624$ sample was recovered via centrifugation, washed with toluene and $\mathrm{CS}_{2}$ . After soaking in a toluene $\phantom{}^{\prime}C S_{2}$ mixture $(50\%$ by volume,
$3{\times}10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ was dried under vacuum at $\mathbf{1}2\mathbf{0}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ for the use in the next cycle.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Design and Synthesis of Ligand. We started the synthesis work with the electron-deficient pentafluorobenzaldehyde monomer which can easily be transformed to the polydentate porphyrin ligand. The synthesis of polyfluorinated ligands with several pyrazoles as terminal groups is challenging and suffers from low yield.23 Here, we report a simple reaction where the 5,10,15,20- tetrakis $(2,3,5,6\cdot$ -tetrafluoro- $^{4}$ -( $\mathrm{i}H.$ -pyrazol- $^{\cdot}4$ -yl)phenyl)- porphyrin nickel (4, TTFPPP, Scheme 1) with four perfluorophenylene units was synthesized from commercially available 2,3,5,6-tetrafluorobenzaldehyde and $^4$ -iodopyrazole. The pyrrole reacted with $^{2,3,5,6-}$ tetrafluorobenzaldehyde to form the fluorinated free base porphyrin. The obtained product, was further metallized by nickel acetate, generating the tetrakis $(2,3,5,6-$ tetrafluorophenyl)-porphyrin nickel 1 in a total yield of $20\%$ . In the meanwhile, the $\mathsf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ bond in $^4$ -iodo-pyrazole was protected with a trityl unit producing compound 2, which was then subjected to a palladium-catalyzed direct cross-coupling reaction with 1 to form the intermediate 3 in a $75\%$ yield. This coupling reaction was conducted in the presence of $t$ -BuLi and $\mathsf{C u C l}$ , which enabled the synthesis of $^3$ in high yield under mild conditions. After that, the compound $^3$ was treated with trifluoroacetic acid to remove the trityl groups, and subsequently replaced by the tert-butyloxycarbonyl (Boc) group. Finally, the Boc group was removed after heating in a mixture of DMF and MeOH for $4^{8\mathrm{h}}$ , resulting in the
final product 4. Matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization time-of-flight spectrometry (MALDI-TOF) was employed to determine the molecular weights of intermedia compound and TTFPPP (Figures $S_{1^{-}}S_{7},$ , see Supporting Information).
Synthesis and Characterization of PCN-624. We constructed the PCN-624 using $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\right]$ cluster as knots and
5,10,15,20-tetrakis $(2,3,5,6$ -tetrafluoro- $^{\cdot}4$ -( $1H.$ -pyrazol-4- yl)phenyl)-porphyrin (TTFPPP) as linkers (Figure 1). The reaction was conducted under solvothermal conditions in DMF at 120 $^{\circ}C$ for $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ . The $\mathrm{PCN}{-}624$ was obtained as a purple crystalline powder in a yield of $74\%$ and characterized by various methods. FT-IR spectrum displayed that PCN-624 exhibited a stretching vibration band at 1622 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ that was assigned to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{N}}$ bond (Figure S8). Thermal gravimetric analysis demonstrated that $P C N-624$ was stable up to 400 $^{\circ}C$ under $\Nu_{2}$ (Figure S9). Powder Xray diffraction (PXRD) measurement revealed that PCN624 adopted an ftw-a topology, which is consistent with that of PCN-602 and simulated results (Figures 2a and Table S1). Each face of the cubic cages in PCN-624 was consisted of a fluorinated porphyrin ligand (Figure 1c, d). The average Ni−Ni distance between opposite faces is 19.8 Å, and the inner void volume was 3332 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ . The window size in the resulting framework is about $6.6\times14.5\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ , which is large enough for some bulky substrates to diffuse inside this framework. To evaluate the porosity of PCN624, we performed $\Nu_{2}$ sorption measurement at 77 K. PCN-624 exhibited $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes of $678\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 1 atm(Figure 2b), and the experimental Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area is calculated as high as 2010 $\mathrm{~m~}^{2}\mathrm{~g~}^{-1}$ . The pore size distribution calculated by using the nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT) method resulted in a pore size of $\mathbf{1.9\nm}$ (Figure 2c), which is close to the theoretical value. Interestingly, PCN$624$ also exhibited high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities of 3.10 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at
$273\mathrm{K}$ and 1.73 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 298 K (Figure S10). The isosteric heat of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was calculated as high as $34.6~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ (Figure S11). It can be attributed to the high electronegativity of fluorine atoms, which mostly promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption through electrostatic interactions. The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption capacity of PCN624 was 0.27 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and 0.13 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $_{273}\mathrm{K}$ and 298 K, respectively (Figure $\mathbf{S12}$ ). Using pure-component isotherm fits, we evaluated the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ selectivity based on the ideal absorbed solution theory (IAST). The IAST selectivity was calculated as 39.7 at 1 bar (Figure $\mathrm{S}_{13}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ).
Chemical Stability. Owing to the strong coordination bond between ${\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}}$ and the pyrazolide groups, and the high connectivity number of $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}\right]$ clusters, PCN-624 was expected to possess excellent chemical stability. To investigate its chemical stability, we dispersed PCN-624 samples in different solutions, including water, ethanol, HCl (1M), $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ $(\mathsf{2M})$ , and ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ $(\mathsf{2M})$ aqueous solutions. After soaking in these solutions for $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ , the PXRD patterns of these treated $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ samples are almost unchanged, which suggests the maintenance of its crystallinity (Figure 2d). As shown in Figure 2e, the residue weight percentage of the PCN-624 samples remained very high $(>96\%)$ in these harsh conditions. In water and ethanol, $P C N-624$ exhibited almost no weight loss $({<}5\mathrm{wt\%})$ . Under the ultra-harsh conditions of aqueous acid $(\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\rho}}_{1}M}\ \mathrm{HCl})$ ) and base $(2\mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3})$ ) solutions, the residual weight percentages were $94~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $95\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. To further confirm the $\mathrm{PCN}{-}624$ networks were intact, we conducted $\Nu_{2}$ sorption measurements for these samples after different treatments. $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms of these treated $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ have minimal deviations from the one of the pristine sample (Figure 2f and Figure $\mathsf{S14}$ ). Moreover, $\mathsf{P C N-}624$ displays much higher chemical stability than its counterpart PCN-602 in acidic aqueous solution, which might be attributed to the high hydrophobicity of its fluorinated cages. To the best of our knowledge, PCN-624 exhibits the high stability in the broadest $\mathsf{p H}$ range among previously reported porphyrinic MOFs despite its extremely large linker. Even $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ samples were soaked in pH harsh conditions for four weeks, they still remained high crystallinity and porosity (Figures S15−S17). This pyrazolide-based MOF with elongated linkers successfully realizes the combination of large porosity and extraordinary chemical stability, which would be of great potential for application in nanoscale chemistry.
Encapsulation Studies. Among the various fullerene derivatives, their bisadducts as electron acceptors have shown outstanding photo conversion efficiency, especially in the field of organic solar cells. For example, the indene$C_{60}$ bisadduct (ICBA) has been widely employed as a typical kind of high-performance acceptor material in polymer solar cells.24 However, the mono-, bis- and tris- ${C}_{60}\mathrm{{\overline{{{\Gamma}}}}}$ - adducts are always obtained as a mixture during synthesis and have to be separated by tedious column chromatography. Therefore, it is of great significance to develop an efficient approach to synthesize and separate desired bis$C_{60}$ -adducts from their mixtures. The $\mathrm{PCN-}624$ network contains cubic cages with highly electron-deficient surface, which has an inner void volume of $3243\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ (Figure S18). According to Rebek’s rule, these cages could selectively encapsulate guest molecules with volumes around $\mathbf{178}3\mathrm{\AA}^{3}$ . Compared with other fullerene adducts, the $C_{60}-$ anthracene bisadduct $(C_{60}\mathrm{An}_{2})$ with a volume of 1802 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3}$ was calculated as the optimum guest molecule among numerous derivatives (Table S2). The ellipsoidal $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ molecule has a minor diameter of $6.4\mathring\mathrm{~A~}$ and a major diameter of $\mathbf{1.1\nm}$ .25 The window size $(6.6{\times}14.5\mathring\mathrm{A}^{2})$ of PCN$624$ is large enough to accommodate the entry and exit of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ . Based on the electron-deficient nanopores, we utilized $\mathrm{PCN-}624$ to trap $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ by dispersing the crystalline powder into the toluene solution at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The capture process of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ was monitored closely by electronic absorption spectra (Figures S19 and S20). Timedependent spectral change demonstrates that the maximum absorption band at $323~\mathrm{nm}$ decreased with the passage of time. After $_{36\mathrm{h}}$ , the residual $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ concentration in the solution was lower than 0.1 mmol ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ . This result suggests that over $86\%$ of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ was captured by PCN624. As control experiments, the adsorption of $C_{60}$ monomer and $C_{60}$ -anthracene monoadduct $(C_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An})$ was conducted under the same conditions. However, no evidence of the encapsulation of $C_{60}$ and $\mathrm{C_{6o}A n}$ was observed from the UV spectra because the volume of $C_{60}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}$ is not high enough to occupy the void optimally. In addition, the additional PXRD patterns of $\mathrm{C_{6o}A n_{2}c P C N-624}$ also indicate the encapsulation of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ in the framework (Figure S21). Meanwhile, the fluorine-free PCN-602 was also employed as a host material for the capture of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ yet very low encapsulation levels were detected (Figures $S_{22}\rangle$ ). Therefore, the combination of suitable void volume and electron-deficient inner surface in PCN-624 required to achieve the efficient capture of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ .
Selective Synthesis of $\mathbf{C_{6o}A n_{2}}$ . Encouraged by the excellent $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ capture performance of PCN-624, we further attempted to employ this network as a nanoreactor to selectively synthesize $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ other than other products (Figure 3). The Diels−Alder reaction between $C_{60}$ and anthracene was conducted in acetonitrile in the presence of PCN-624. Both of $C_{60}$ and anthracene alone cannot form any host−guest inclusion with $P C N-624$ , which can be attributed to the poor solubility of $C_{60}$ and anthracene in acetonitrile.  In addition, control experiments between $C_{60}$ and anthracene without PCN-624 or in the presence of the fluorine-free PCN-602 in acetonitrile at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ cannot produce the $C_{60}$ -anthracene adducts. Interestingly, when we added PCN-624, fullerene, and anthracene in acetonitrile and heated the mixture at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 h, and then filtered and soaked the residual $P C N-624$ in toluene, high-performance liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (HPLC-MS) measurement  revealed the $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ bisadduct was generated as the only product (Figures $S23$ ) All of these results indicated that PCN-624 might be promising as a catalytic nanoreactor for the efficient synthesis of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ bisadduct.
In order to optimize the synthetic conditions, we attempted to investigate the effects of a range of factors, including solvents, substrate ratios and reaction time (Table 1). The yield of all the products was determined by standard working methods (Figures $S_{24-539})$ ). And all the products were analyzed by elemental analysis (Table $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ). Without any catalyst in toluene, the reaction between $C_{60}$ and anthracene generated a mixture of ${\mathrm{C}}_{6\mathrm{o}}{\mathrm{An}}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ and trisadduct $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{3}$ in the yields of $23\%$ , $19\%$ , and $3\%$ , respectively (Table 1, entry 1). However, very little product was detected in acetonitrile owing to the poor solubility of both substrates (Table 1, entry 2). In the presence of $P C N-624$ in toluene, the Diels−Alder reaction still produced a mixture of three adducts with similar yields after 12 h and $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Table 1, entries 3 and 4), suggesting that the introduction of PCN-624 made no difference in the homogeneous reactions. Interestingly, when we conducted the reaction in acetonitrile with $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ after 12 h, only bisadduct $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ was formed in $43\%$ yield (Table 1, entry 5). We ascribed this result to the remarkable capture ability of $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ toward the bisadduct $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ , which supplies sustained chemical driving force in the heterogeneous catalysis process. When the reaction time was prolonged to $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ , slightly lower yields of $40\%$ and $38\%$ were obtained owing to the retro-Diels−Alder reaction (Table 1, entries 6 and 7). The highest yield of $52\%$ was obtained by increasing the ratio of $C_{60}$ and anthracene after 12 h (Table 1, entry 8). As control experiments, the non-fluorinated PCN-602 was employed in acetonitrile and toluene, while no obvious catalytic effect was observed (Table 1, entries 9 and 10). Therefore, $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ appears to enable the Diels−Alder reaction between anthracene and $C_{60}$ to selectively generate the bisadduct, whereas the substrates would not ordinarily react at all under the same conditions. We infer that the inner nanoenvironment of PCN-624 favors the formation of the labile $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ product, generated in a thermodynamically controlled reaction between $C_{60}$ and anthracene.
Recyclability of PCN-624. Cycling performance of heterogeneous catalysts is crucial for practical applications. Interestingly, the PCN-624 can be easily separated and recovered from the reaction mixture through filtration and washing with solvents for next cycles. The regenerated PCN-624 can be subjected to the next round catalytic synthesis of $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ bisadduct. After five cycles, the PCN-624 network still retained its crystallinity and porosity, which were confirmed by its PXRD patterns and $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ (Figures $S_{40-S_{42}})$ . In addition, in the presence of the recycled PCN-624, the yield of the $\mathrm{C}_{6\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{An}_{2}$ almost has no difference with that of the original cycle (Figure $\mathrm{S}43\mathrm{,}$ ). All of the results demonstrated that $\mathsf{P C N-}624$ could well maintain its high catalytic activity and selectivity.
In addition, the light-harvesting nature of porphyrins made them quite promising for photocatalytic applications. In nature, one of the well-known porphyrin assemblies is the antenna complex in the photosynthetic lightharvesting system. To realize artificial photosynthesis, MOFs are one kind of ideal scaffolds to assemble different functional molecules. There have been many examples of MOFs for light harvesting and photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and hydrogen evolution.24 As in most catalytic systems, heterogeneous photocatalysts are easily separated from the reaction mixtures for recycling use. However, it remains a challenge to develop highly stable MOFs in most catalytic reactions. Combining the intrinsic lightharvesting properties of porphyrins and the extraordinary stability, $\mathrm{PCN}_{-624}$ is one of the best choices for practical photocatalytic applications on account of its ability to be recovered and reused. In the future work, we will further explore the light-harvesting and photocatalytic performance of the pyrazolide-based MOFs, especially in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, proton reduction, and organic transformations.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we have developed a pyrazolide-based fluorinated MOF network, which presented a desirable cavity with electron-deficient walls. Owing to the strong coordination bond, the high connectivity of its nodes and its hydrophobic inner surface, PCN-624 exhibited excellent chemical stability in both acid and base conditions. In addition, the electron-deficient nature together with the optimal pore size and pore volume supply a strong driving force to selectively bind particular fullerene derivatives. This unveiled a new efficient method for the purification and the selective synthesis of fullerene adducts with rationally designed MOFs. Considering the availability of various linkers with different sizes and electronic properties, the creation of electron-deficient interfaces provides a new chemical platform for structural and functional designs. Therefore, these advancements open new perspectives in the design of MOFs with specific functionalities for the sustainable production of fine chemicals.
